I've been trying not to join this thread, but I can't ignore this.

Code still is a valued, protected asset. Anyone who writes code values
it, and anyone who uses someone else's code values it (if it's any
good). Protection schemes are now so stupidly intrusive that they
actually damage your hardware. A small minority of programmers become
unimaginably rich. Plenty of others make a living (and make other
people rich).

The reason you didn't need to go and find an open source alternative
for that boxed software you saw in the store was that the boxed
software was reasonably priced. It didn't screw up your system (if you
used it carefully) and it certainly didn't invade your privacy or
insult you with legally flawed EULAs.

You didn't look for a cross-platform equivalent because there weren't
any other platforms (to which you would have access, anyway). I also
hail from the days when your most useful bit of memory was the cassette
buffer.

Things have changed. Commercial software is complex, bug-ridden and
phenomenally expensive. It's a huge business, and the economics of it
now bear no relation to its value. It is an industry whose customer
service is record-breakingly poor (with a few honourable exceptions),
because the customer's contract is with the retailer, not the
developer. I almost feel sorry for the retailers.

Open source alternatives now exist precisely to offer competition to
this - open source is effectively a product of the free market.

You're welcome to keep your code closed source, but please bear in mind
that almost all roguelikes are open source, and many of the people who
play them appreciate the significance of this. So try not to rubbish
the open source concept as some sort of johnny-come-lately fad.

While I'm here, your other error was to pretend that the .NET framework
is some sort of helpful, user-friendly standard which we should all
embrace. Again, you're welcome to code in whatever language you like,
but some audience research will help you avoid releasing the wrong
product to the wrong people.

Don't get bothered by the cheating stuff though, it really doesn't
matter. You're helping people play the way they want to play, which is
fine. 

